WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.688 --> 00:00:05.010
(crowd chattering indistinctly)

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.140
- So good afternoon everyone.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.690
I'm Kim Goff-Crews and it is my privilege

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:13.524
to welcome you here today
as we honor our veterans.

00:00:13.524 --> 00:00:15.360
Now, before we begin, I would like

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.060
to thank the Yale Police
Department Color Guard

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.680
for presenting the colors

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.920
and express our deep gratitude
to the Quinnipiac people

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.873
for this land on which we stand.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:28.770
It is my pleasure to warmly
welcome the students,

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:32.580
faculty, staff, and alumni
who are with us today

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.583
who have served and who are
preparing to serve our country.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.280
We are grateful for the freedoms

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.073
and privileges that your
sacrifices have made possible.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.360
Each member of our veterans
community contributes

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.251
to the vibrant culture of our university.

00:00:48.251 --> 00:00:50.400
During our ceremony, you will hear

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.130
from a veteran leader
and a current student,

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.680
and we will honor a former staff person

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.623
who has supported and
advocated for Yale veterans.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.850
Our speakers and our
honoree represent commitment

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:05.580
to honor and country to our veterans

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.970
and to those who care for
them and support them.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:10.710
So we will start and I will now,

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:13.330
my pleasure to introduce
President Peter Salovey.

00:01:14.405 --> 00:01:17.572
(audience applauding)

00:01:22.015 --> 00:01:26.265
(Peter and Kim speak indistinctly)

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.630
- Thank you, Vice President Goff-Crews.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:32.670
Appreciate that introduction.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.798
Thank you all for being here,

00:01:34.798 --> 00:01:37.920
and it gives me great pleasure
to see so many members

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.040
of the Yale community gathered

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.760
for our commemoration of Veterans Day.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:49.760
It's a privilege especially
to extend a warm welcome

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.350
to current and former servicemen

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:54.753
and servicewomen who
are here with us today.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:58.076
Why don't we recognize
all those in the service?

00:01:58.076 --> 00:02:02.076
(Peter and audience applauding)

00:02:06.508 --> 00:02:08.880
So for over three
centuries, Yale students,

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.390
faculty, staff, and alumni have answered,

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:16.173
dutifully and with
distinction, the call to serve.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:18.690
The university's proud tradition

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:23.520
of service began with
the Revolutionary War

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.730
and it includes nearly 30,000 Yalies

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.880
who served in the First
and Second World Wars

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.223
and in Korea and in Vietnam.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:36.993
And now the tradition continues.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.610
It continues through
those who are enlisted

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:44.790
in the Reserve Officers'
Training Corps on campus,

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:48.600
as well as the many
veterans in our community.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.440
And ceremonies like this one remind us

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.390
of the tremendous sacrifices
our veterans have made

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:57.510
to keep the peace in a world

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.513
that has become a more dangerous place.

00:03:01.857 --> 00:03:04.266
In the words of former Secretary of State

00:03:04.266 --> 00:03:06.870
and Yale honorary degree recipient,

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.007
the late Madeleine Albright,

00:03:09.007 --> 00:03:11.227
"While democracy in the long run

00:03:11.227 --> 00:03:14.047
"is the most stable form of government,

00:03:14.047 --> 00:03:17.517
"it is in the short run
among the most fragile."

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.880
The price of liberty is noted

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.410
by the eminent Wendell Phillips

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:26.940
and many others is internal vigilance.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.250
Around us, threats to democracy,

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.460
stoked increasingly by disinformation

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.710
and the division that it sows,

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:37.233
demand our utmost vigilance.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:44.193
Standing watch are
those who sit before us.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.850
Every veteran here today understands

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:51.180
that the essential values
in which we believe

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:54.753
so deeply require sacrifice.

00:03:55.702 --> 00:03:57.900
In thinking of how the
veterans assembled here

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.170
this afternoon exemplify
our highest ideals,

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:04.620
I'm reminded of what Yale looks for

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.560
and more particularly whom it looks for.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:12.510
Several decades ago, University
President Kingman Brewster

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:16.260
memorably distilled the
Yale admissions process as,

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:20.077
this his words, "a combination
of looking for those

00:04:20.077 --> 00:04:23.827
"with a zest to stretch
the limits of their talents

00:04:23.827 --> 00:04:27.393
"and those with an
outstanding public motivation.

00:04:28.597 --> 00:04:31.383
"We have to make the
hunchy choice," he said,

00:04:32.782 --> 00:04:37.027
"as to whether or not the
candidate for admission is likely

00:04:37.027 --> 00:04:40.287
"to be a leader in whatever
they end up doing."

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:43.590
So let there be no doubt

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:46.260
that with each of our Yale veterans,

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:49.290
that with each of you who have set out

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:53.073
to improve the world for
this and future generations,

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.440
we got it right.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.290
Yale made the right decision.

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.990
You have rewarded our hunch

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:03.450
and most importantly, you
have represented the best

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:06.183
of humanity with your heroism.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:10.560
And so for this, you have
our deepest gratitude.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:12.060
You have our deepest admiration

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.150
for bringing great honor
to this university.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:18.349
Of course, at Yale, we are
ever aware of our obligation

00:05:18.349 --> 00:05:21.090
to pair our words in support of veterans

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:24.270
with action that provides it.

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:27.570
Yale, I'm proud to say,
is routinely recognized

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:30.720
among the best colleges in
the nation for veterans,

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.480
in part due to our commitment to ensuring

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:38.010
that a Yale education is
affordable for everyone,

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:39.930
such as through our participation

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:43.350
in the Yellow Ribbon
program, just as an example.

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:48.120
We strive also to honor
and support our servicemen

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:51.300
and women through the
Yale Veterans Network,

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:54.480
a campus group for
students, faculty and staff,

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:59.010
and the Yale Veterans
Association for all our alumni.

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:01.380
And beyond our campus meanwhile,

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:02.820
the Yale Law School is helping

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:06.390
to rid the veterans benefit
system of racial disparities.

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:09.750
We have many from the
law school here today.

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:11.550
By supporting our veterans,

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:14.550
we affirm our unending
appreciation to them

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.590
for strengthening our society,

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:19.680
indeed sustaining our society,

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:23.190
through their service and sacrifice.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:28.190
So I wanna close by shifting
from Yale's efforts to yours.

00:06:30.967 --> 00:06:34.350
"The ultimate test of
a person's conscience,"

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:36.847
said Senator Gaylord Nelson,

00:06:36.847 --> 00:06:41.077
"may be their willingness
to sacrifice something today

00:06:41.077 --> 00:06:43.327
"for future generations

00:06:43.327 --> 00:06:46.767
"whose words of thanks will not be heard."

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:52.440
So today, let our words
of thanks ring out loudly

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:54.964
to reach those veterans in our community

00:06:54.964 --> 00:06:57.455
who have passed this test

00:06:57.455 --> 00:07:00.601
and the service members on campus

00:07:00.601 --> 00:07:03.393
who have enlisted to join them.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.570
The valor of Yale's veterans is a source

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:10.440
of tremendous pride to this university,

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:14.250
and I want to thank you all once more

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:17.850
for being here today and for helping us

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:21.240
to honor them and you.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:22.821
Thank you very much.

00:07:22.821 --> 00:07:25.988
(audience applauding)

00:07:37.327 --> 00:07:42.327
(band performing "The
Star-Spangled Banner")

00:08:53.935 --> 00:08:56.280
- [Sergeant] Color Guard, order.

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:59.030
(rifles tapping)

00:09:04.096 --> 00:09:05.679
Ceremonial at ease.

00:09:06.699 --> 00:09:07.782
- Sorry, sir.

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:13.320
Good afternoon, I'm Violetta Ware

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:15.180
co-chair of the Yale Veterans Network

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:17.460
and I'm here to recite the invocation.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:20.583
If those of you who could stand,
please stand at this time.

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:26.970
Grand Architect of the
Universe, called by many names,

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:29.670
in many languages, by many voices,

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:32.730
today we ask that your
guiding hands hold us

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:36.390
and all sisters and brothers
in military service near.

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:39.060
We are grateful for all who now serve,

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:43.020
all who have served,
and all for those who,

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:45.397
in the words of President Abraham Lincoln,

00:09:45.397 --> 00:09:47.490
"gave the last full measure of devotion"

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:49.530
in service of their country.

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:52.290
We are joined in humility, gratitude,

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:54.390
and our own measure of devotion,

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:56.580
knowing that as we seek to understand

00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:59.160
an increasingly complex world,

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:01.050
we are sheltered by the vigilant service

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:03.360
of citizens to our republic.

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:06.510
We ask special regard
for those who have served

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:09.630
but who have not yet been
found and returned home,

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:11.670
and for those who have returned

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:14.820
but who have yet to
find themselves at home.

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:16.920
We honor our military and ask

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:19.980
that whatever is required
of them be informed

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:23.400
by your infinite wisdom,
the light and the truth

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:25.590
as they honorably and loyally seek

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:29.100
to create a peaceful and
secure future for us all.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:30.573
Àṣẹ, you may be seated.

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:42.263
- All right.

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:44.693
Whew.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:47.400
Good afternoon everyone.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:50.460
First I'd like to recognize
and thank President Salovey.

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:55.460
Secretary Goff-Crews, Provost
Strobel, Dean Pericles Lewis,

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:58.833
Chaplain Saltiel, and the
Veterans Day Committee.

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:02.919
I'd like to personally
thank Dean Risa Sodi,

00:11:02.919 --> 00:11:06.924
Ms. Patricia Wei, Mr. Jack Beecher,

00:11:06.924 --> 00:11:09.720
Haad Hyder, Dean Chandhoke,

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:11.580
and all of my fall-term professors

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:12.690
because they will inevitably

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:14.794
influence my GPA in a few weeks.

00:11:14.794 --> 00:11:16.500
(audience laughing)

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:18.690
Most importantly, I'd like to thank

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:21.512
the veterans, reservists,
National Guardsmen,

00:11:21.512 --> 00:11:24.900
active-duty service members,
civil service members,

00:11:24.900 --> 00:11:27.570
firefighters, law enforcement personnel,

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:30.120
and their families who willingly sacrifice

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:32.040
and endure more than most will ever know

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:33.780
for being in attendance.

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:35.790
To everyone else, thank you for being here

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:37.090
and supporting this event.

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:40.140
It's truly an honor to have been selected

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:41.550
and given the responsibility

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:43.639
to provide my remarks here today.

00:11:43.639 --> 00:11:46.841
Although I will describe what
Veterans Day means to me,

00:11:46.841 --> 00:11:49.110
I wanna emphasize that I do not intend

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:51.930
to have the last word on
the meaning of Veterans Day

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:54.570
or how we should observe or honor it.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:57.870
Today my only goals are to
underscore the importance

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:01.230
of unity in our society,
thank those that have had

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:04.500
and will continue to have a
profound impact on my life,

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:06.360
and honor the sacrifices of veterans

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:07.953
and uniform service members.

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:13.410
To me, Veterans Day is
not meant to be exclusive.

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:15.810
It is not a day for
civilians to thank veterans

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:17.820
and uniform service members,

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:20.280
and it is certainly not a day for veterans

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:22.770
and service members to
rest on their laurels,

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:26.790
bask in America's gratitude
and become complacent.

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:30.690
To me, Veterans Day is
inclusive to all Americans

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:33.454
and it is meant to remind us
of the importance of unity.

00:12:33.454 --> 00:12:36.570
It is a day for all Americans
to acknowledge the fact

00:12:36.570 --> 00:12:38.430
that America's freedom was achieved

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:40.830
and is maintained through
the selfless sacrifices

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:44.430
that have transcended and
continue to transcend every group

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:46.889
and every class of American society.

00:12:46.889 --> 00:12:50.130
Each year, Veterans Day
provides an opportunity

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:51.990
for civilians to thank those

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:54.000
who have served for their sacrifices,

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.550
but simultaneously, it provides veterans

00:12:56.550 --> 00:12:59.820
and service members with a
chance to thank the civilians,

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:03.363
mentors, friends, and family
members that have supported

00:13:03.363 --> 00:13:06.782
and continue to support them
throughout their journeys.

00:13:06.782 --> 00:13:10.470
For those of us who have served
and are currently serving,

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:13.020
this day should remind
us to lead by example,

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:14.700
keep striving for greatness,

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:16.860
and live our lives in a way
that would make those we knew

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:19.710
who paid the ultimate sacrifice proud.

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:21.450
But perhaps most importantly,

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:23.280
Veterans Day is meant to remind us all

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:27.060
that freedom is not free, it
is realized through action

00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:29.430
and it can only be maintained
when, as Americans,

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:32.130
we unify and collectively
support each other

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:34.050
in our efforts to improve our society

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:35.350
and create a better world.

00:13:36.376 --> 00:13:38.700
Before I get into my military service

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:41.070
and my path to becoming a veteran at Yale,

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:43.620
I'd like to share a bit about myself.

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:45.300
I grew up in southern Minnesota,

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:47.460
in a town of less than 2000 people

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:51.420
with my mother, father,
sister, and brother.

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:53.220
My siblings and I had a tough upbringing

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:55.680
in what I would consider
to be a broken home.

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:59.070
We had little money, my parents
were addicted to opioids

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:00.270
and different forms of abuse

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:02.640
were common occurrences in my household.

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:04.680
I've often been asked about the decisions

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:06.810
and actions that I undertook to persevere

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:09.150
through adversity in my childhood,

00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:10.500
what made me different than those

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:11.880
who went through similar struggles

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:14.160
but ended up in different places,

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:16.740
and what lessons I've
learned along the way.

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:19.770
In response to those questions,
I always say two things.

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:22.290
First, when faced with adversity,

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:24.480
leaders do what needs
to be done regardless

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:27.414
of how unpleasant those
actions and decisions may be.

00:14:27.414 --> 00:14:30.000
The second and more
important thing I say is

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.223
that there's no such thing as self-made.

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:34.560
When I needed to get away

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:36.490
or have a place to stay for a night,

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:38.550
it was the Borniki and Westfall families

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:40.110
that opened their homes and provided me

00:14:40.110 --> 00:14:42.090
with the support I needed.

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:45.030
I was not different, I was not unique.

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:46.710
I did not persevere through the adversity

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:48.840
of my adolescence on my own.

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:51.270
Leaders in my community rallied around me.

00:14:51.270 --> 00:14:53.110
They gave me encouragement
during dark times

00:14:53.110 --> 00:14:56.400
and they held me accountable
when I needed it most.

00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:58.020
It took a village to build me up

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:00.600
and show me pathways to success,

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:03.901
and I am forever grateful for
their support and leadership.

00:15:03.901 --> 00:15:07.080
My experiences in the
military were no different.

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:09.330
In 2010, I joined the military

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:10.980
to provide support for my family

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:13.830
and create a promising future for myself.

00:15:13.830 --> 00:15:16.140
But shortly after enlisting, I learned

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:19.133
that personal and professional
success would not come easy.

00:15:19.133 --> 00:15:21.840
I spent the first two years
of my military service

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:23.850
in training where I struggled

00:15:23.850 --> 00:15:25.779
to build strong personal connections,

00:15:25.779 --> 00:15:27.407
failed far more than I succeeded

00:15:27.407 --> 00:15:29.550
in my professional endeavors,

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:31.290
and neglected to reach out and be there

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:33.938
for my family who I
claim to be supporting.

00:15:33.938 --> 00:15:36.360
It wasn't until I arrived at my first unit

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:38.850
at Fort Bragg, North Carolina that I began

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:40.710
to achieve more consistent success

00:15:40.710 --> 00:15:42.460
and gain perspective that I needed.

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:44.850
When I arrived at Fort Bragg,

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:47.910
I met two individuals that
forever changed my life,

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:50.654
one who is here today and one who is not.

00:15:50.654 --> 00:15:52.320
The one who is with us here today

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:55.503
was Second Lieutenant Chris
Brown, now Major Brown.

00:15:56.520 --> 00:16:01.080
When I arrived at my unit, I
was arrogant, loud, selfish.

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:04.290
I wasn't a good friend,
I wasn't a good teammate,

00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:06.270
and I definitely wasn't a leader.

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:08.760
Like the others, Chris had every reason

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:10.500
to distance himself from me,

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:12.777
but instead he coached, challenged,

00:16:12.777 --> 00:16:15.439
and mentored me in all areas of my life.

00:16:15.439 --> 00:16:17.820
His methods were often non-traditional,

00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:19.800
but they were effective.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:21.750
From politely encouraging me

00:16:21.750 --> 00:16:23.970
to put on a gas mask and body armor

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:27.180
and run a half marathon at
lunch in 100-degree heat,

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:29.490
to quizzing me on radio
communications procedures

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:32.130
that were last relevant
during World War II,

00:16:32.130 --> 00:16:34.590
he taught me a few valuable lessons.

00:16:34.590 --> 00:16:37.350
By providing me with a
half marathon for lunch,

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:39.330
he taught me that you have
to do what others won't

00:16:39.330 --> 00:16:41.280
to achieve what others don't.

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:43.380
By running those half marathons with me,

00:16:43.380 --> 00:16:45.120
he showed me that as a leader,

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:46.830
you can never ask somebody to do something

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:48.843
that you wouldn't do yourself.

00:16:48.843 --> 00:16:51.690
And by quizzing me on the
communications techniques

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:54.120
of World War II soldiers, he taught me

00:16:54.120 --> 00:16:56.790
that you can learn
something from everyone.

00:16:56.790 --> 00:16:58.680
Chris taught me how to be a friend,

00:16:58.680 --> 00:17:01.500
teammate, and leader,
but most importantly,

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:03.450
he taught me what it means to be a man.

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:04.906
Thank you, Chris.

00:17:04.906 --> 00:17:08.906
(Bobby and audience applauding)

00:17:10.621 --> 00:17:12.720
The second person I met at Fort Bragg

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:15.240
was Staff Sergeant Brandon Cullen-Towle.

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:17.640
Brandon and I had a lot of similarities

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:18.840
and he was one of the only people

00:17:18.840 --> 00:17:20.910
that I talked openly with about my past

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:22.770
and the loss of my father.

00:17:22.770 --> 00:17:24.390
After telling Brandon about my reason

00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:27.069
for joining the military and
my commitment to my family,

00:17:27.069 --> 00:17:28.890
he held me accountable and encouraged me

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:31.290
to be more involved with
my mother and siblings.

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:33.699
Upon noticing my inability

00:17:33.699 --> 00:17:36.930
to build strong personal
connections and friendships,

00:17:36.930 --> 00:17:39.630
Brandon invited me to weekend gatherings

00:17:39.630 --> 00:17:42.150
and made me a part of his friend group.

00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:44.400
And he advocated for me
as an asset to the unit

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:46.500
and the broader military family community.

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:49.500
Nevertheless, Brandon
and I's friendship ended

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:52.650
on August 25th, 2012 when he passed away

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:55.851
in a motorcycle accident in
Spring Lake, North Carolina.

00:17:55.851 --> 00:17:58.110
Brandon served three tours in Afghanistan,

00:17:58.110 --> 00:18:00.060
once receiving the Purple Heart.

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:02.640
Brandon was and is known
for the profound impact

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:05.274
that he had on the lives
of everyone he knew.

00:18:05.274 --> 00:18:07.114
And today, his influence continues

00:18:07.114 --> 00:18:09.390
to positively affect those that knew him

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:10.640
and those that never did.

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:14.160
Brandon's sister,
Kaylee, and Brother, Kam,

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:15.363
are with us here today.

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:18.720
And each year in Dover, New Hampshire,

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:20.730
their family organizes a memorial event

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:23.640
and provides a scholarship
in Brandon's name.

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:26.430
Brandon's impact in life
and death should remind us

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:27.930
that the way we live our lives

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:29.820
and the imprints we put on this earth

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:32.430
are lasting, they have meaning,

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:34.260
and they could change someone else's life.

00:18:34.260 --> 00:18:36.588
Thank you for being here
today, Kaylee and Kam.

00:18:36.588 --> 00:18:40.588
(Bobby and audience applauding)

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:44.550
I share these two stories with you today

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:47.610
because Chris and Brandon
exemplify the selflessness,

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:49.587
empathy, and leadership that most veterans

00:18:49.587 --> 00:18:51.330
and service members leverage

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:53.550
to create impact on a daily basis.

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:55.500
Outside of selfless sacrifice

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:57.510
and defense of freedom and democracy,

00:18:57.510 --> 00:19:00.150
veterans provide two
broad things to society:

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:02.150
leadership and perspective.

00:19:02.150 --> 00:19:06.016
Regardless of branch,
occupation, or combat experience,

00:19:06.016 --> 00:19:09.897
veterans appreciate and embody
transformational leadership.

00:19:09.897 --> 00:19:11.850
They give their subordinates the space

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:14.520
and responsibility to succeed or fail,

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:15.750
but they're always around and willing

00:19:15.750 --> 00:19:18.060
to build their people back up.

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:20.160
Veterans know that when
you dare to be great,

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:22.050
you either win or you learn.

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:23.340
They know and believe this

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:25.500
because their lives depended on it.

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:28.740
As leaders, veterans prioritize empathy

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:30.600
and individualized consideration.

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:31.890
And when times get tough,

00:19:31.890 --> 00:19:34.641
veterans never let others
struggle on their own.

00:19:34.641 --> 00:19:38.070
Struggle and suffering
are necessary for growth

00:19:38.070 --> 00:19:41.730
and throughout life, every
person is subjected to both.

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:43.290
But when these unpleasant conditions

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:45.600
leave one lost and without direction,

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:47.730
veterans are often uniquely positioned

00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:50.373
to provide the perspective
that most do not have.

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:52.740
Veterans understand that no matter

00:19:52.740 --> 00:19:54.040
what you're going through,

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:58.410
no matter how difficult and
troubling your situation may be,

00:19:58.410 --> 00:20:00.750
there is someone else who
went through something worse

00:20:00.750 --> 00:20:02.850
and they probably dealt
with it more gracefully

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:04.646
than you are currently.

00:20:04.646 --> 00:20:07.440
These selfless individuals
have an intimate understanding

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:09.960
of hardship, and when veterans integrate

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:12.960
into the civilian populace,
they benefit the collective

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:16.015
by eliminating defeatism
and creating hope.

00:20:16.015 --> 00:20:19.590
Living veterans provide
significant value to our society.

00:20:19.590 --> 00:20:21.540
But it is the fallen
veterans that provide us

00:20:21.540 --> 00:20:24.510
with an example of what
we should aspire to be.

00:20:24.510 --> 00:20:27.480
Fallen veterans remind us to be selfless

00:20:27.480 --> 00:20:30.540
and to prioritize the
mission and the people.

00:20:30.540 --> 00:20:32.820
But perhaps most
importantly, they remind us

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:35.370
that we could be far greater
than we currently are.

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:40.740
Today in 2023, our society
is in need of unity

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:43.020
and one way to work toward
achieving that unity is

00:20:43.020 --> 00:20:45.870
by bridging the military-civilian divide.

00:20:45.870 --> 00:20:48.330
In my opinion, places like Yale University

00:20:48.330 --> 00:20:51.060
offer the perfect environment
to work toward that goal

00:20:51.060 --> 00:20:53.340
and unity can be realized
through interactions

00:20:53.340 --> 00:20:55.290
between Yale's traditional students

00:20:55.290 --> 00:20:57.870
and the university's veteran community.

00:20:57.870 --> 00:21:00.718
Yale's traditional students
challenge veterans in many ways,

00:21:00.718 --> 00:21:02.730
and they're critical in forcing veterans

00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:04.230
to consider new perspectives

00:21:04.230 --> 00:21:06.230
and embrace innovative ways of thinking.

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:11.130
That said, it is not lost on
me that as I stand here today,

00:21:11.130 --> 00:21:13.350
I'm in the presence of
some of America's heroes.

00:21:13.350 --> 00:21:15.480
And each day as I walk around this campus,

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:17.550
I'm thankful that Yale's
traditional students

00:21:17.550 --> 00:21:20.040
have the opportunity to learn from them.

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:22.950
Heroes like Chris Hallows, Jimmy Hatch,

00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:26.310
Connor McEvoy, Danny McCoslin, Will Myers,

00:21:26.310 --> 00:21:28.137
and Steve Scheffer are some
of the greatest veterans

00:21:28.137 --> 00:21:29.790
and active-duty service members

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:31.410
that our country has to offer,

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:33.510
and they provide more
experienced knowledge,

00:21:33.510 --> 00:21:35.430
and expertise to Yale's
traditional students

00:21:35.430 --> 00:21:37.230
than I could put into words.

00:21:37.230 --> 00:21:41.220
However, these men and the
many male and female veterans

00:21:41.220 --> 00:21:43.890
that I did not mention offer much more.

00:21:43.890 --> 00:21:48.570
They're sons, daughters,
brothers, sisters,

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:52.440
husbands, wives, fathers, and mothers

00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:54.690
and in my opinion, they
can teach us a whole lot

00:21:54.690 --> 00:21:57.490
about how to live a wholesome
life and be a good person.

00:21:58.650 --> 00:22:02.010
To the ROTC cadets and students aspiring

00:22:02.010 --> 00:22:03.450
to serve in attendance,

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:05.460
I wanna personally thank
you for your selflessness

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:07.680
and willingness to serve our country.

00:22:07.680 --> 00:22:09.120
After Yale, you will have the option

00:22:09.120 --> 00:22:11.580
to do whatever you want,
and with a Yale degree,

00:22:11.580 --> 00:22:13.260
you'll be choosing one
of the most difficult

00:22:13.260 --> 00:22:15.240
but rewarding paths.

00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:17.250
Serving the United States of America

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:18.930
was the greatest responsibility

00:22:18.930 --> 00:22:21.690
and opportunity that I've ever been given,

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:24.765
and I hope that in a few
years you feel the same way.

00:22:24.765 --> 00:22:27.430
The world is filled with
chaos and uncertainty,

00:22:27.430 --> 00:22:28.890
and we should all be thankful

00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:30.810
that there are people like you willing

00:22:30.810 --> 00:22:32.670
to answer our nation's call.

00:22:32.670 --> 00:22:35.160
You are all heroes in your own right

00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:36.720
and I hope you know that.

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:38.714
Thank you all for your time.

00:22:38.714 --> 00:22:41.110
(audience applauding)
- Color guards,

00:22:41.110 --> 00:22:44.860
(Sergeant indistinct) Halt.

00:22:48.828 --> 00:22:51.995
(Sergeant indistinct)

00:22:56.658 --> 00:23:00.325
(trumpeters perform "Taps")

00:23:44.265 --> 00:23:47.336
(shoes clattering)

00:23:47.336 --> 00:23:48.336
- Thank you.

00:23:49.561 --> 00:23:50.963
All right, you may be seated.

00:23:57.840 --> 00:23:59.640
I'm still feeling that, are you?

00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:00.473
Yeah.

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:05.820
So the university recognizes
outstanding contributions

00:24:05.820 --> 00:24:10.620
to Yale veterans with the Annual
Veterans Day Tribute Award.

00:24:10.620 --> 00:24:15.570
The recipient of the 2023
Veterans Day Tribute Award is

00:24:15.570 --> 00:24:18.690
to John Jack Beecher.

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:20.940
Now, the reality is most of us know Jack,

00:24:20.940 --> 00:24:22.770
so he really doesn't
really need a introduction,

00:24:22.770 --> 00:24:24.720
but I'm gonna give him one anyway.

00:24:24.720 --> 00:24:27.660
One, because we wanna
make sure that he knows

00:24:27.660 --> 00:24:30.090
how we see him and what he's meant to us.

00:24:30.090 --> 00:24:31.950
And the second is because
we want a written record

00:24:31.950 --> 00:24:34.503
in our archives of who
he was and what he meant.

00:24:35.482 --> 00:24:40.200
Jack is a United States Air Force veteran,

00:24:40.200 --> 00:24:43.650
retired Yale business
leader, university citizen,

00:24:43.650 --> 00:24:46.500
and a longtime champion
for veterans at Yale.

00:24:46.500 --> 00:24:49.290
He is with us today with
his family, welcome,

00:24:49.290 --> 00:24:53.043
and we are thrilled to publicly
recognize his contributions.

00:24:54.090 --> 00:24:59.090
In 2016, Jack became the inaugural
veterans liaison at Yale.

00:25:00.090 --> 00:25:03.000
His charge from Provost Ben Polak,

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:04.770
whom I see in the audience,

00:25:04.770 --> 00:25:06.630
was to create and implement a plan

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:10.113
to support university-affiliated
military veterans.

00:25:11.190 --> 00:25:13.383
Now, the job was intended to be part-time,

00:25:13.383 --> 00:25:16.170
but those of you who are
familiar with Jack know

00:25:16.170 --> 00:25:18.810
that there was nothing
part-time about his dedication.

00:25:18.810 --> 00:25:22.080
He was the first central
point of contact for student,

00:25:22.080 --> 00:25:24.693
faculty, staff, and
alumni military veterans.

00:25:25.860 --> 00:25:29.850
Jack became a champion of
veterans and a problem solver.

00:25:29.850 --> 00:25:31.410
He supported staff veterans

00:25:31.410 --> 00:25:34.230
through the innovative
Veterans Count campaign

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:37.530
and created important connections
with our student veterans,

00:25:37.530 --> 00:25:40.800
notably those in the Eli
Whitney program in Yale College

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:42.420
and the veterans enrolled
in the Law School

00:25:42.420 --> 00:25:43.670
and School of Management.

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:47.760
At the time, student veterans
felt underrepresented

00:25:47.760 --> 00:25:51.630
and unheard, and Jack
became their advocate.

00:25:51.630 --> 00:25:54.210
He listened and acted on their behalf,

00:25:54.210 --> 00:25:55.650
building a network of leaders

00:25:55.650 --> 00:25:59.193
and staff across the university
who valued all veterans.

00:26:00.030 --> 00:26:02.070
This resulted in policy changes,

00:26:02.070 --> 00:26:03.870
advocacy, and relationships

00:26:03.870 --> 00:26:05.400
that improve the university's environment

00:26:05.400 --> 00:26:07.743
for veterans, veteran students, and staff.

00:26:09.210 --> 00:26:12.030
As you all know, some of our
military-affiliated students

00:26:12.030 --> 00:26:13.230
are preparing to serve through

00:26:13.230 --> 00:26:17.040
Yale's Naval and Air Force ROTC programs.

00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:19.260
Many are here with us today.

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:22.472
Jack has been an unwavering
supporter of ROTC

00:26:22.472 --> 00:26:24.900
and especially welcoming and helpful

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:28.200
when new commanding
officers arrived on campus.

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:30.045
He has also worked extensively

00:26:30.045 --> 00:26:33.120
with the Yale Veterans Network,

00:26:33.120 --> 00:26:35.430
which is our staff
veterans' affinity group,

00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:39.330
and with the Yale Veterans
Association, our veteran alumni,

00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:41.940
once again creating positive relationships

00:26:41.940 --> 00:26:43.763
between veterans across the university

00:26:43.763 --> 00:26:46.688
to improve culture and experiences.

00:26:46.688 --> 00:26:49.350
As President Salovey mentioned,

00:26:49.350 --> 00:26:52.470
two years ago, U.S. News
&amp; World Report ranked Yale

00:26:52.470 --> 00:26:54.720
the number one school in the country

00:26:54.720 --> 00:26:58.051
for veterans and
active-duty service members.

00:26:58.051 --> 00:27:00.180
While this was a recognition

00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:03.003
of great collective effort
across the university,

00:27:03.870 --> 00:27:05.898
the ranking was due in no small part

00:27:05.898 --> 00:27:08.310
to the role that Jack played

00:27:08.310 --> 00:27:09.990
in helping to create the environment

00:27:09.990 --> 00:27:13.110
in which veterans,
active-duty service members,

00:27:13.110 --> 00:27:17.223
and student participants in
ROTC are valued and respected.

00:27:18.810 --> 00:27:21.240
Jack, if you can come up.

00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:23.280
On behalf of our entire university

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:24.300
and especially our veterans,

00:27:24.300 --> 00:27:26.880
I wanna thank you for
all of you contributed

00:27:26.880 --> 00:27:29.605
to create a strong community of respect,

00:27:29.605 --> 00:27:32.160
connection, and belonging
for our veterans.

00:27:32.160 --> 00:27:34.470
This is an award that's so well deserved.

00:27:34.470 --> 00:27:37.370
So I'm gonna give you a small
token and maybe say remarks.

00:27:38.730 --> 00:27:40.403
One second, let me give you this token.

00:27:45.671 --> 00:27:47.153
Thank you, Jack.

00:27:47.153 --> 00:27:49.364
- Thank

00:27:49.364 --> 00:27:52.531
(audience applauding)

00:27:58.672 --> 00:27:59.880
(Jack sighs)

00:27:59.880 --> 00:28:04.563
- President Salovey, Secretary
Goff-Crews, officers of Yale,

00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:07.980
US military veterans and their families,

00:28:07.980 --> 00:28:11.553
and other honored guests,
Happy Veterans Day.

00:28:12.510 --> 00:28:15.240
Thank you for being here
to acknowledge the service

00:28:15.240 --> 00:28:17.802
of our veterans and their families.

00:28:17.802 --> 00:28:19.860
Well, you've already heard more about me

00:28:19.860 --> 00:28:21.630
than you probably want to know.

00:28:21.630 --> 00:28:23.778
I'm going to tell you more.

00:28:23.778 --> 00:28:24.780
(audience chuckling)

00:28:24.780 --> 00:28:28.830
During my first attempt at
college, I was a marginal student

00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:31.050
as I was unable to balance my need

00:28:31.050 --> 00:28:34.080
for fun with academic realities.

00:28:34.080 --> 00:28:38.490
I withdrew, and while I like
to call it a tactical retreat,

00:28:38.490 --> 00:28:40.203
it was actually an escape.

00:28:41.250 --> 00:28:43.410
I enlisted in the Air Force

00:28:43.410 --> 00:28:48.240
and served from November
1965 to July of 1969.

00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:50.640
To put that in historical perspective,

00:28:50.640 --> 00:28:52.920
Neil Armstrong, a Navy veteran,

00:28:52.920 --> 00:28:55.530
and Buzz Aldrin, an Air Force veteran,

00:28:55.530 --> 00:28:59.085
walked on the moon within
weeks of my return.

00:28:59.085 --> 00:29:02.370
Kingman Brewster, a Navy veteran,

00:29:02.370 --> 00:29:05.100
was the president of Yale, and to date,

00:29:05.100 --> 00:29:07.860
the only veteran to have
served in that capacity,

00:29:07.860 --> 00:29:10.892
however, I'm told, we're
getting another president soon.

00:29:10.892 --> 00:29:12.716
- You never know.
- Never know.

00:29:12.716 --> 00:29:15.000
(audience laughing)

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:17.880
After receiving my honorable discharge,

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:20.446
I was told not to wear my uniform home

00:29:20.446 --> 00:29:23.190
on the flight from Seattle.

00:29:23.190 --> 00:29:27.870
At that time, many Americans
blamed veterans for the war.

00:29:27.870 --> 00:29:31.020
And while I know now that
it was misdirected anger,

00:29:31.020 --> 00:29:32.850
I didn't know it then.

00:29:32.850 --> 00:29:35.790
It was painful and it was embarrassing,

00:29:35.790 --> 00:29:38.010
and I felt insulted that my service

00:29:38.010 --> 00:29:40.650
had been disavowed and canceled.

00:29:40.650 --> 00:29:43.410
I didn't discuss my military
experience for many years

00:29:43.410 --> 00:29:45.210
and could never have imagined

00:29:45.210 --> 00:29:47.460
that I'd be standing before you today

00:29:47.460 --> 00:29:49.770
at Yale honoring veterans.

00:29:49.770 --> 00:29:53.100
I've learned a great deal
in the ensuing years.

00:29:53.100 --> 00:29:56.880
After the 9/11 attacks on
New York and Washington,

00:29:56.880 --> 00:29:59.280
I was concerned with safety at Yale

00:29:59.280 --> 00:30:02.430
and my first thought was to
find the veterans on campus

00:30:02.430 --> 00:30:06.010
because I knew they could
be called on to stand watch

00:30:07.800 --> 00:30:11.910
and that they would understand
and respond and serve.

00:30:11.910 --> 00:30:14.760
In 2001, we had no way to do this.

00:30:14.760 --> 00:30:17.520
Today we are more connected than ever.

00:30:17.520 --> 00:30:20.880
With encouragement from relentless alumni

00:30:20.880 --> 00:30:25.020
from the late 1960s, you
all know who you are,

00:30:25.020 --> 00:30:27.390
and the support from
our administration led

00:30:27.390 --> 00:30:29.943
by the daughter of a Navy admiral,

00:30:31.380 --> 00:30:33.360
we've come a long way.

00:30:33.360 --> 00:30:37.680
ROTC units celebrated a
decade back on campus.

00:30:37.680 --> 00:30:40.590
There's a full-time university liaison

00:30:40.590 --> 00:30:42.870
for Veterans and Military Affairs

00:30:42.870 --> 00:30:45.330
and teams from admissions,
the Dean's Office,

00:30:45.330 --> 00:30:47.250
the Registrar, Financial Aid,

00:30:47.250 --> 00:30:51.810
and our Office of Diversity,
Inclusion and Accessibility

00:30:51.810 --> 00:30:54.180
have extended themselves to make Yale

00:30:54.180 --> 00:30:58.740
the university of choice
for many veterans.

00:30:58.740 --> 00:31:02.430
The Warrior Scholar Program
was founded at Yale.

00:31:02.430 --> 00:31:05.790
It supports enlisted
veterans who, like me,

00:31:05.790 --> 00:31:08.100
had little awareness of
their future potential

00:31:08.100 --> 00:31:10.590
until their military service.

00:31:10.590 --> 00:31:13.500
And while I didn't have
the Warrior Scholar Program

00:31:13.500 --> 00:31:17.940
available to me, I did meet
a lovely nurse from New York

00:31:17.940 --> 00:31:21.067
who said, "Jack, you're a
really good softball player,

00:31:21.067 --> 00:31:23.997
"but have you ever thought
of going back to college?"

00:31:25.080 --> 00:31:28.620
I did go back for my degree
and I also married her,

00:31:28.620 --> 00:31:32.640
and we will be celebrating
our 53rd anniversary

00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:34.080
on November 29th.

00:31:34.080 --> 00:31:36.145
Thank you for everything, Barbara.

00:31:36.145 --> 00:31:39.395
(audience applauding)

00:31:41.340 --> 00:31:43.020
I'm fond of saying that veterans

00:31:43.020 --> 00:31:44.700
are better by being at Yale,

00:31:44.700 --> 00:31:48.090
that Yale is better by having
veterans studying here,

00:31:48.090 --> 00:31:51.333
and our country is made
much stronger by both.

00:31:52.288 --> 00:31:57.288
The common bond created
by service to others,

00:31:57.630 --> 00:32:01.920
to our community, and to
our country, is strong

00:32:01.920 --> 00:32:06.060
and runs deep at Yale and in the military.

00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:11.040
This bond is intergenerational
and self-perpetuating.

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:15.160
Veterans and alumni willingly
accept responsibility

00:32:16.320 --> 00:32:20.130
to current students and active military.

00:32:20.130 --> 00:32:23.220
This spring, I was contacted by a member

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:26.700
of the class of 1968 wanting to know

00:32:26.700 --> 00:32:30.253
how he could support our
undergraduate veterans.

00:32:30.253 --> 00:32:32.760
Interestingly enough, he had served

00:32:32.760 --> 00:32:36.570
in the army a few miles
from Pleiku Air Base

00:32:36.570 --> 00:32:39.210
at the same time I was stationed there.

00:32:39.210 --> 00:32:41.760
He traveled from the Midwest to New Haven

00:32:41.760 --> 00:32:45.180
to meet me and two of
our veteran students.

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:48.093
After dinner, he sent
a note, which I quote,

00:32:49.567 --> 00:32:52.477
"Both of them are truly unique individuals

00:32:52.477 --> 00:32:56.257
"and quite extraordinary
in their own ways.

00:32:56.257 --> 00:33:00.720
"They make me proud to be a
vet who graduated from Yale."

00:33:00.720 --> 00:33:05.720
That's from a 1968 graduate
in the darkest part

00:33:06.210 --> 00:33:09.726
of the university's
relationship with the military.

00:33:09.726 --> 00:33:12.180
They make me proud as well.

00:33:12.180 --> 00:33:14.160
The intersection of Yale in the military

00:33:14.160 --> 00:33:18.300
has incalculable
potential to improve both.

00:33:18.300 --> 00:33:21.330
I'm proud of all that
has been accomplished,

00:33:21.330 --> 00:33:25.083
but this being Yale, I know
the best is yet to come.

00:33:25.950 --> 00:33:28.380
Personally, I've only
expected three things

00:33:28.380 --> 00:33:30.510
from my military service:

00:33:30.510 --> 00:33:33.299
the respect of those who served before me,

00:33:33.299 --> 00:33:36.453
the respect of those who served after me,

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:41.760
and a flag on my coffin, which in my case,

00:33:41.760 --> 00:33:46.620
will be the same one used to
drape my father-in-law's casket

00:33:46.620 --> 00:33:49.743
as he was a World War II
veteran of the Army Air Corps.

00:33:50.700 --> 00:33:53.190
I've received so much more
than that from my service,

00:33:53.190 --> 00:33:56.970
including GI Bill support to
complete my college education,

00:33:56.970 --> 00:34:00.160
and most importantly,
an understanding of duty

00:34:00.160 --> 00:34:05.160
and responsibility to
myself and to others.

00:34:05.280 --> 00:34:07.950
Today, I thank you for your
support of our veterans,

00:34:07.950 --> 00:34:11.580
that special 11% of our
population who pledge

00:34:11.580 --> 00:34:15.210
to defend our constitution
with their lives.

00:34:15.210 --> 00:34:17.160
They understand what it means to be part

00:34:17.160 --> 00:34:19.079
of something larger themselves,

00:34:19.079 --> 00:34:23.029
and they support those who
followed in the same path.

00:34:23.029 --> 00:34:24.870
It's very difficult for me

00:34:24.870 --> 00:34:27.210
to describe my emotions on days like this.

00:34:27.210 --> 00:34:30.360
I burst with pride in seeing the women

00:34:30.360 --> 00:34:33.660
and the men in our ROTC programs,

00:34:33.660 --> 00:34:38.160
the next generation preparing
to protect and defend us.

00:34:38.160 --> 00:34:41.100
And my interactions with
undergraduate veterans

00:34:41.100 --> 00:34:44.130
and active military studying here fill me

00:34:44.130 --> 00:34:47.544
with optimism for America's future.

00:34:47.544 --> 00:34:50.571
Their presence is a validation

00:34:50.571 --> 00:34:54.153
of all veterans' decisions to serve.

00:34:55.170 --> 00:34:58.230
In recent years, I've
been thanked many times

00:34:58.230 --> 00:35:00.300
for my service to my country.

00:35:00.300 --> 00:35:02.700
While visiting friends in Wisconsin,

00:35:02.700 --> 00:35:05.284
I had a chance meeting with an Iowa farmer

00:35:05.284 --> 00:35:07.350
who is a Vietnam veteran.

00:35:07.350 --> 00:35:09.810
We talked briefly and found

00:35:09.810 --> 00:35:12.810
that we were in country at the same time.

00:35:12.810 --> 00:35:16.747
When we parted, he said to
me, with tears in his eyes,

00:35:16.747 --> 00:35:18.177
"Welcome home, brother."

00:35:19.080 --> 00:35:24.080
I feel at home and welcome at
Yale as everyone should be.

00:35:24.510 --> 00:35:26.280
I thank you for the opportunity

00:35:26.280 --> 00:35:28.560
to serve the veteran community here.

00:35:28.560 --> 00:35:30.276
Happy Veterans Day.

00:35:30.276 --> 00:35:33.443
(audience applauding)

00:35:59.414 --> 00:36:03.164
(uplifting brass band music)

00:37:24.359 --> 00:37:29.359
(audience applauding)
(music continuing)

00:38:03.399 --> 00:38:08.399
(audience applauding)
(music continuing)

00:38:09.949 --> 00:38:13.532
(music drowns out singing)

00:38:34.858 --> 00:38:39.853
(audience applauding)
(music continuing)

00:38:39.853 --> 00:38:43.436
(music drowns out singing)

00:38:52.964 --> 00:38:57.964
(crowd chattering indistinct)
(music continuing)

00:39:05.253 --> 00:39:10.253
(audience applauding)
(music continuing)

00:39:12.341 --> 00:39:17.341
(audience members hooting)
(music continuing)

00:39:21.918 --> 00:39:25.501
(music drowns out singing)

00:39:43.389 --> 00:39:47.639
(audience applauding and cheering)

00:39:51.750 --> 00:39:54.033
- Please stand as you're willing and able.

00:39:56.760 --> 00:40:00.390
May we go forth from this
place of profound memory

00:40:00.390 --> 00:40:04.920
and deep meaning with
grateful hearts and souls,

00:40:04.920 --> 00:40:06.929
grateful for the sacrifice

00:40:06.929 --> 00:40:10.320
of our veterans and their families.

00:40:10.320 --> 00:40:12.240
Let us never forget all they have done

00:40:12.240 --> 00:40:14.973
to bravely protect
freedom and human rights.

00:40:16.080 --> 00:40:19.620
From the moment our veterans
make the commitment to serve,

00:40:19.620 --> 00:40:20.910
they were committing to a life

00:40:20.910 --> 00:40:23.310
that would forever be changed.

00:40:23.310 --> 00:40:25.170
May we be mindful that the life

00:40:25.170 --> 00:40:28.680
of a veteran is always multidimensional.

00:40:28.680 --> 00:40:32.021
It is rooted in the pride
and promise of liberation,

00:40:32.021 --> 00:40:34.920
coupled with the ever present possibility

00:40:34.920 --> 00:40:37.023
of peril or alienation.

00:40:38.010 --> 00:40:40.170
All of these things are held in tension

00:40:40.170 --> 00:40:43.200
and contain a longing for
a certain kind of peace

00:40:43.200 --> 00:40:45.363
to revive our weary world.

00:40:46.530 --> 00:40:48.420
As we honor our veterans and we seek

00:40:48.420 --> 00:40:51.060
to grow closer to this deep peace,

00:40:51.060 --> 00:40:56.060
may we embody a peace born
from wisdom and understanding,

00:40:56.460 --> 00:41:00.330
a peace nurtured with
compassion and mercy,

00:41:00.330 --> 00:41:04.350
a peace strengthened by
courage and commitment.

00:41:04.350 --> 00:41:08.190
And may we be blessed
this day and every day

00:41:08.190 --> 00:41:11.700
by the shimmering light
of our God of many names,

00:41:11.700 --> 00:41:16.350
our God who creates, sustains,
and embraces all life.

00:41:16.350 --> 00:41:17.183
Amen.

00:41:18.944 --> 00:41:21.861
(shoes clattering)

00:41:22.860 --> 00:41:24.480
- We're gonna close in a
second so you can sit down.

00:41:24.480 --> 00:41:27.573
I only have a few notes
before we sign off.

00:41:28.860 --> 00:41:31.140
So before we go, I do wanna take a moment

00:41:31.140 --> 00:41:32.700
to wish very happy birthday

00:41:32.700 --> 00:41:35.520
to the United States
Marine Corps, Semper Fi.

00:41:35.520 --> 00:41:38.270
We know it's your birthday,
so congratulations. (chuckles)

00:41:38.270 --> 00:41:40.140
(audience applauding)

00:41:40.140 --> 00:41:41.790
I also want to make sure that you know

00:41:41.790 --> 00:41:44.940
that there will be a
Yale Veterans Association

00:41:44.940 --> 00:41:48.420
Military History at Yale
Tour right after this,

00:41:48.420 --> 00:41:51.600
a walking tour that can
accommodate about 25 people.

00:41:51.600 --> 00:41:54.150
It'll be leaving from the
cenotaph right behind me.

00:41:55.140 --> 00:41:57.450
And I actually wanna thank
the planning committee

00:41:57.450 --> 00:41:58.740
and our ceremony participants.

00:41:58.740 --> 00:42:00.935
So thank you very much
for all of that you did.

00:42:00.935 --> 00:42:03.720
(Kim and audience applauding)

00:42:03.720 --> 00:42:06.120
And special thanks to Tom Duffy

00:42:06.120 --> 00:42:08.730
and the Yale School of
Music Brass Ensemble

00:42:08.730 --> 00:42:12.270
for sharing with music
inspiring, energetic.

00:42:12.270 --> 00:42:16.020
Thank you very much.
(audience applauding)

00:42:16.020 --> 00:42:16.853
We're gonna learn all,

00:42:16.853 --> 00:42:18.842
some of us are gonna
learn all the songs, okay?

00:42:18.842 --> 00:42:20.310
(audience chuckling)

00:42:20.310 --> 00:42:22.320
So when today has passed and we return

00:42:22.320 --> 00:42:23.550
to our very busy lives,

00:42:23.550 --> 00:42:28.020
I hope that we will continue
to remember the people of Yale

00:42:28.020 --> 00:42:31.740
who have faithfully taken
oath to protect and serve.

00:42:31.740 --> 00:42:34.050
We are a stronger, more diverse,

00:42:34.050 --> 00:42:36.360
and more inclusive
community because of them.

00:42:36.360 --> 00:42:40.652
And I wanna thank you for
joining us, have a great day.

00:42:40.652 --> 00:42:43.819
(audience applauding)

00:42:47.437 --> 00:42:50.687
(gentle music chiming)

